/* std/ops — the operator traits (language-completeness 5, `plan/traits.md` 13).

   Declaring one of these for a `datac`/`enum` gives the type the operator, and
   the front end lowers the operator into the trait method before borrow
   checking and code generation: with `impl Add for T`, `a + b` is `a.add(&b)`;
   with `impl Ord for T`, `a < b` is `a.cmp(&b) < 0`; and `-a` with `impl Neg`
   is `a.neg()`. Both operands are evaluated once, left to right, and are only
   borrowed, so `a + b` leaves `a` and `b` owned. Builtin numeric types keep
   their builtin operators: the desugar runs only where the builtin check would
   already have failed.

   There are deliberately **no blanket impls**, exactly as `std/convert` has
   none, and no operator is derived: a type that wants one writes the impl. */

pub trait Add {
    fun Self.add(rhs: &Self).R: Self
}

pub trait Sub {
    fun Self.sub(rhs: &Self).R: Self
}

pub trait Mul {
    fun Self.mul(rhs: &Self).R: Self
}

pub trait Div {
    fun Self.div(rhs: &Self).R: Self
}

pub trait Rem {
    fun Self.rem(rhs: &Self).R: Self
}

pub trait Neg {
    fun Self.neg().R: Self
}

// The order trait: `cmp` returns the sign of the order (negative, zero,
// positive), like `memcmp`. It supplies `<`, `<=`, `>` and `>=` and not
// equality: `==`/`!=` come from a declared `eq` (`plan/syntax.md` 5).
pub trait Ord {
    fun Self.cmp(other: &Self).R: i32
}
